SUMMARY:The Declaration of Independence: The Signers
DESCRIPTION:As part of the America 250 (https://bloomington.iu.edu/campus
 -life/events/america-250/) initiative\, the Lilly Library is proud to pre
 sent an expansion of our Declaration of Independence exhibition\, focusin
 g on the 56 signatories.  IU Libraries Lilly Library invites you to The D
 eclaration of Independence: The Signers\, an exhibition of letters and do
 cuments bearing the autographs of the fifty-six men who signed the August
  2\, 1776 handwritten copy of the Declaration. Though the length and cont
 ent of these letters and documents vary widely\, from brief notes or orde
 rs to long and detailed personal letters\, they all offer insight into th
 e daily lives\, activities\, and concerns of the signers. One aspect of t
 heir lives that these documents don't reveal\, however\, is the ownership
  of slaves by a great many of the signers\, which is discussed in the "Ra
 ce\, Slavery\, and Native Americans" section of the exhibition in the Lil
 ly Library's North Gallery\, The Declaration of Independence: The Motives
 \, The Moment. (https://libraries.indiana.edu/lilly-library/declaration-i
 ndependence)  Documents signed by fifty-four of the fifty-six signers are
  displayed in the Lilly Library's South Gallery. Documents signed by the 
 two other signers—Philip Livingston of New York and James Smith of Penn
 sylvania—are on display in the North Gallery in The Declaration of Inde
 pendence: The Motives\, The Moment.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  As part of the <a href="https://bloomin
 gton.iu.edu/campus-life/events/america-250/" target="_blank" rel="noopene
 r">America 250</a> initiative\, the Lilly Library is proud to present an 
 expansion of our Declaration of Independence exhibition\, focusing on the
  56 signatories.</p><p>  IU Libraries Lilly Library invites you to <em>Th
 e Declaration of Independence: The Signers</em>\, an exhibition of letter
 s and documents bearing the autographs of the fifty-six men who signed th
 e August 2\, 1776 handwritten copy of the Declaration. Though the length 
 and content of these letters and documents vary widely\, from brief notes
  or orders to long and detailed personal letters\, they all offer insight
  into the daily lives\, activities\, and concerns of the signers. One asp
 ect of their lives that these documents don't reveal\, however\, is the o
 wnership of slaves by a great many of the signers\, which is discussed in
  the "Race\, Slavery\, and Native Americans" section of the exhibition in
  the Lilly Library's North Gallery\, <em><a href="https://libraries.india
 na.edu/lilly-library/declaration-independence" target="_blank" rel="noope
 ner">The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment.</a></em><
 /p><p>  Documents signed by fifty-four of the fifty-six signers are displ
 ayed in the Lilly Library's South Gallery. Documents signed by the two ot
 her signers—Philip Livingston of New York and James Smith of Pennsylvan
 ia—are on display in the North Gallery in <a href="https://libraries.in
 diana.edu/lilly-library/declaration-independence" target="_blank" rel="no
 opener"><em>The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment</em
 ></a>.</p>
