LOCATION:LILLY LIBRARY
GEO:39.167909;-86.518974
SUMMARY:First Thursday Declaration of Independence Lecture
DESCRIPTION:As part of our yearlong America 250 programming\, IU Associat
 e Professor of History Konstantin Dierks will give a talk entitled "Reson
 ances of the Declaration of Independence in American History."  Is it wor
 th commemorating the Declaration of Independence in 2026? Does the Declar
 ation still have resonance in our time of terrible crisis? The Declaratio
 n expressed ideals with intensely contested and severely limited meanings
  in 1776. Yet those brittle ideals somehow carried forward through Americ
 an history. Looking closely at the complexities of 1776 can help us ponde
 r how the Declaration might speak to our crisis in 2026.  Before and afte
 r the talk\, please feel free to peruse our Declaration of Independence e
 xhibitions: The Motives\, The Moment and The Signers.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  As part of our yearlong America 250 pro
 gramming\, IU Associate Professor of History Konstantin Dierks will give 
 a talk entitled "Resonances of the Declaration of Independence in America
 n History."</p><p style="padding-left: 40px\;">  Is it worth commemoratin
 g the Declaration of Independence in 2026? Does the Declaration still hav
 e resonance in our time of terrible crisis? The Declaration expressed ide
 als with intensely contested and severely limited meanings in 1776. Yet t
 hose brittle ideals somehow carried forward through American history. Loo
 king closely at the complexities of 1776 can help us ponder how the Decla
 ration might speak to our crisis in 2026.</p><p>  Before and after the ta
 lk\, please feel free to peruse our <em>Declaration of Independence</em> 
 exhibitions: <em>The Motives\, The Moment</em> and <em>The Signers</em>.<
 /p>
