BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261121
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261122
RECURRENCE-ID;VALUE=DATE:20261121
LOCATION:LILLY LIBRARY
GEO:39.167909;-86.518974
SUMMARY:The Declaration of Independence: The Signers
DESCRIPTION:IU Libraries Lilly Library invites you to The Declaration of 
 Independence: The Signers\, an exhibition of letters and documents bearin
 g the autographs of the fifty-six men who signed the August 2\, 1776 hand
 written copy of the Declaration. Though the length and content of these l
 etters and documents vary widely\, from brief notes or orders to long and
  detailed personal letters\, they all offer insight into the daily lives\
 , activities\, and concerns of the signers. One aspect of their lives tha
 t these documents don't reveal\, however\, is the ownership of slaves by 
 a great many of the signers\, which is discussed in the "Race\, Slavery\,
  and Native Americans" section of the exhibition in the Lilly Library's N
 orth Gallery\, The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment.
  (https://libraries.indiana.edu/lilly-library/declaration-independence)  
 Documents signed by fifty-four of the fifty-six signers are displayed in 
 the Lilly Library's South Gallery. Documents signed by the two other sign
 ers—Philip Livingston of New York and James Smith of Pennsylvania—are
  on display in the North Gallery in The Declaration of Independence: The 
 Motives\, The Moment.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  IU Libraries Lilly Library invites you 
 to <em>The Declaration of Independence: The Signers</em>\, an exhibition 
 of letters and documents bearing the autographs of the fifty-six men who 
 signed the August 2\, 1776 handwritten copy of the Declaration. Though th
 e length and content of these letters and documents vary widely\, from br
 ief notes or orders to long and detailed personal letters\, they all offe
 r insight into the daily lives\, activities\, and concerns of the signers
 . One aspect of their lives that these documents don't reveal\, however\,
  is the ownership of slaves by a great many of the signers\, which is dis
 cussed in the "Race\, Slavery\, and Native Americans" section of the exhi
 bition in the Lilly Library's North Gallery\, <em><a href="https://librar
 ies.indiana.edu/lilly-library/declaration-independence" target="_blank" r
 el="noopener">The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment.<
 /a></em></p><p>  Documents signed by fifty-four of the fifty-six signers 
 are displayed in the Lilly Library's South Gallery. Documents signed by t
 he two other signers—Philip Livingston of New York and James Smith of P
 ennsylvania—are on display in the North Gallery in <a href="https://lib
 raries.indiana.edu/lilly-library/declaration-independence" target="_blank
 " rel="noopener"><em>The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The M
 oment</em></a>.</p>
